 for one of my students from Summit.

8
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,260
 And if we're being honest,

9
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:20,840
 most of the time when we attend a graduation

10
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:22,640
 or commencement ceremony,

11
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:25,280
 what we really care about is the presentation

12
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:29,220
 of the diploma or the degrees, right?

13
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:33,280
 Which unfortunately usually happens at the end.

14
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:34,440
 But during the ceremony,

15
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:36,560
 so in addition to the main speakers,

16
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:39,320
 they actually have five or six more students

17
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:41,520
 giving different kind of speeches.

18
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:45,440
 (speaking in foreign language)

19
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:06,560
 And so when it got to the third or the fourth speakers,

20
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:08,680
 I started noticing that some of the younger kids

21
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:11,620
 in the room, they began to lose their patience.

22
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:13,040
 And their parents, on the other hand,

23
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,840
 were struggling to try to keep them quiet.

24
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:19,360
 Some families, they even had to leave the room entirely.

25
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:21,640
 And so by the time all the speeches ended

26
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:25,000
 and the presentation of diplomas finally began,

27
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,880
 more than one hour had already passed.

28
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:31,880
 (speaking in foreign language)

29
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:49,320
 And so this experience sort of reminds me

30
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:51,880
 that in a world where we usually assume

31
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:54,360
 that more is always better,

32
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:57,800
 more money, more power, more efficiency,

33
